      "text": "Advanced membrane filtration methods, such as nanofiltration and reverse osmosis, are capable of intercepting PFAS molecules via size- exclusion and/or Donnan effect [25,26] , but the nano/sub-nanometer scaled pores require high operating pressures (5 \u2013 55 bar) and are also highly vulnerable to membrane fouling [27,28] . To address these issues, ion-exchange moieties have been incorporated to porous membrane domain to remove PFAS molecules via adsorption mechanisms [27] . For instance, nanofibrous membranes, with electrospun ultrathin nanofibers ( < 200 nm) and post-grafted anion-exchange moieties [29,30] , removed 90 % of perfluorooctanoic acid at a static pressure of only 0.01 \u2013 0.2 bar, which is significantly less than that required by conventional nano - filtration membranes [31] . Furthermore, the removal process can be controlled by adjusting operating conditions, such as temperature and electric field, allowing for feasible regeneration via switching between adsorption and desorption [32 \u2013 36] . However, concerns regarding sta - bility of nanofibrous membranes (during the treatment and regeneration processes) were maintained, due to the sacrifice of mechanical strength and durability for the loose membrane structure [37] . In addition to membrane stability, the assessment of removal per - formance under complex water chemistry is crucial."
