      "text": "In addition to membrane stability, the assessment of removal per - formance under complex water chemistry is crucial. The environmental pH, for instance, can cause swelling or collapse of the polyanions/pol - ycations in the membrane matrix [38,39] , leading to inadequate inter - action between PFAS molecules and adsorption sites [25,40] . Additionally, the role of ionic strength and co-/counter-charged ions is also important. Co-ions, for example, can compete with negatively- charged PFAS molecules for electrostatic adsorption sites [41] , whereas counter-ions may compress the electrical double layer on the adsorbent [42] . Multivalent cations, such as Mg 2 + and Ca 2 + , have been reported to affect the adsorption process. These ions can form complexes with negatively-charged PFAS molecules, causing an impaired electro - static attraction, but ultimately resulting in a higher molecule size and hydrophobicity [43,44] . These factors, along with membrane stability, must be considered to obtain an accurate assessment of the performance of developed membranes in PFAS removal. This research aimed to synthesize adsorptive membranes for pH- swing adsorption of PFAS molecules using the wet-phase inversion method. Given the negatively-charged nature of major PFAS molecules"
